# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke 12 opens with a crowd: “there were gathered together an innumerable multitude of people, insomuch that they trode one upon another” (12:1). Jesus “began to say unto his disciples first of all, Beware ye of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy” (12:1). He goes on speaking to them through the verses that follow (12:2–12), and Luke records what He says about fear, about confessing Him before men and about the Holy Ghost teaching “in the same hour what ye ought to say” (12:12).'))
A(('p', '2.2 The request. Then a voice comes out of the crowd. “And one of the company said unto him, Master, speak to my brother, that he divide the inheritance with me” (12:13). Luke does not name the man. He does not say who the brother is, what the inheritance is, or whether the man’s claim is just. Jesus answers him: “Man, who made me a judge or a divider over you?” (12:14). Jesus does not do what the man asks. Luke does not say what the man says next.'))
A(('call', 'A Request Comes Before the Story',
   'One of the company asked Jesus to speak to his brother about dividing an inheritance (Luke 12:13). Jesus asked who made Him a judge or a divider over them (12:14), and then spoke a warning to them (12:15, set out word for word in Section 4). Luke says Jesus “spake a parable unto them” after it (12:16). Luke does not say that the story is told about the man who asked, and nothing is added here to make it so.'))
A(('p', '2.3 Where the warning stands. Luke 12:15 begins, “And he said unto them.” It is a warning that Jesus gives before the story and after His answer to the man. The verse is set out word for word in Section 4, where Jesus’ own words are recorded. Luke 12:16 follows directly: “And he spake a parable unto them, saying.” Luke does not narrow “them” to the man who asked, to the disciples or to the multitude, and the text does not say in so many words which of these Jesus means in 12:15 and 12:16.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The setting before the scene. The chapter before it ends with the scribes and the Pharisees urging Jesus “vehemently” and “laying wait for him, and seeking to catch something out of his mouth, that they might accuse him” (11:53–54). Luke 12:1 then says the multitude gathered, and the chapter proceeds from there. Luke does not say that the man who asks about the inheritance is one of the scribes or the Pharisees.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The prophetic context. Jesus’ ministry in the Gospels is a ministry in which He says He was sent to preach the kingdom. In Luke He says, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). Luke 12:16–21 does not use the word kingdom. After the story Jesus tells His disciples, “Fear not, little flock; for it is your Father’s good pleasure to give you the kingdom” (12:32), and the story is read as spoken in that ministry and not as a saying that floats free of it.'))
A(('p', '2.6 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives the occasion: a request about an inheritance, a refusal to be a judge or a divider, a warning, and then “a parable.” Luke does not say that Jesus tells the story as an answer to the man’s request. He says only that Jesus told it after the warning and to “them.”'))
A(('tbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Luke 12:1', '“an innumerable multitude of people”', 'The crowd; Jesus speaks first to His disciples.'],
    ['Luke 12:13', '“Master, speak to my brother, that he divide the inheritance with me”', 'The request of one of the company.'],
    ['Luke 12:14', '“Man, who made me a judge or a divider over you?”', 'Jesus’ answer to the man.'],
    ['Luke 12:16', '“he spake a parable unto them”', 'One parable named; “them” is not narrowed.'],
    ['Luke 4:43', '“I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also”', 'What Jesus says He was sent to do.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The passage is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The ground (12:16). “And he spake a parable unto them, saying, The ground of a certain rich man brought forth plentifully.” The story opens with “a certain rich man” and with his ground. The ground “brought forth plentifully.” The text does not say who the man is, where the ground is, or what it grew.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The man’s question (12:17). “And he thought within himself, saying, What shall I do, because I have no room where to bestow my fruits?” The man speaks, and Luke says he speaks “within himself.” He asks a question, and gives the reason for it: “I have no room where to bestow my fruits.” The text does not say what he has done with the fruits before this, or what other room he has.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The man’s plan (12:18). “And he said, This will I do: I will pull down my barns, and build greater; and there will I bestow all my fruits and my goods.” The plan has three steps. He will pull down his barns. He will build greater. He will bestow there “all my fruits and my goods.” The text does not say how the barns are built or paid for, and it does not say that anyone else is spoken to or consulted.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The man’s words to his soul (12:19). “And I will say to my soul, Soul, thou hast much goods laid up for many years; take thine ease, eat, drink, and be merry.” The man will speak to his own soul. What he will say has four parts: “thou hast much goods laid up for many years”; “take thine ease”; “eat, drink, and be merry.” The text does not say whether he says it, or how long “many years” is.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The word of God (12:20). “But God said unto him, Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?” The story turns on the word “But.” God is named for the first time in the story, and He speaks. He says three things. He calls the man “fool.” He says “this night thy soul shall be required of thee.” He asks, “then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?” The text does not say how God spoke, how the soul is required, or what the man answers. The question is the last thing said inside the story.'))
A(('p', '3.6 What follows the story (12:21). The next verse begins, “So is he that layeth up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God.” These are Jesus’ own words about the story. They are set out word for word in Section 4, where Jesus’ identifications are recorded, and they are not paraphrased here.'))
A(('p', '3.7 What the story says and does not say. The text says a certain rich man had ground that brought forth plentifully. It says he thought within himself, had no room, planned to pull down his barns and build greater, and meant to bestow there all his fruits and goods. It says he would say to his soul that he had much goods laid up for many years and could take his ease. It says God spoke to him that night, called him “fool,” said his soul would be required, and asked whose those things would be. It does not say how the man came to be rich, whether he did anything wrong in getting or keeping the goods, whether he is a certain person or a certain kind of person, or whether he heard God’s word. It does not say what happened to his goods or to him after the question.'))
A(('p', '3.8 What the story leaves unsaid. The story does not say whether the man had family, neighbours or workers. It does not say what “this night” is, what “required” means beyond the word, or who will have the things he provided. It does not say what he should have done instead, and the only contrast it draws is in Jesus’ own closing words in 12:21.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus says around the story. Jesus speaks twice in His own words about the matter, once before the story and once after it. Before the story He says: “Take heed, and beware of covetousness: for a man’s life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth” (Luke 12:15). After the story He says: “So is he that layeth up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God” (12:21). The words inside the story, those of the rich man and those of God (12:17–20), are the story’s own and are not Jesus’ statements of meaning. Everything Jesus identifies is in 12:15 and 12:21, and the table records it in His words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of what Jesus says', 'What Jesus says', 'Verse'],
    ['How He begins the warning', '“Take heed, and beware of covetousness”', 'Luke 12:15'],
    ['What a man’s life does not consist in', '“a man’s life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth”', 'Luke 12:15'],
    ['How He begins after the story', '“So is he that”', 'Luke 12:21'],
    ['What the man does', '“layeth up treasure for himself”', 'Luke 12:21'],
    ['What he is not', '“is not rich toward God”', 'Luke 12:21'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not name in the story. Jesus names covetousness, a man’s life, the abundance of the things he possesses, treasure laid up for himself, and being rich toward God. He leaves the parts of the story without an identification. Jesus does not identify the rich man. Jesus does not identify the ground. Jesus does not identify the fruits or the goods. Jesus does not identify the barns, or the greater barns. Jesus does not identify the soul. Jesus does not identify the “many years” or “this night.” Jesus does not identify the ones whose the things will be. Jesus does not state in so many words that the man who asked about the inheritance is the rich man, or that the rich man has a brother. He says “So is he,” and then names what such a one does. Jesus does not state what “rich toward God” is beyond the words He says.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only covetousness, a man’s life, the abundance of the things he possesses, treasure laid up for himself and being rich toward God, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the rich man is a certain person or class, that the barns, the goods or the soul stand for something, or that the story is told about the man who asked for the inheritance is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.3 The words inside the story stay inside it. The rich man’s words in 12:17–19 and God’s words in 12:20 are part of what Jesus tells. “Thou fool” is God’s address to the man within the story. The text does not say that Jesus applies the word to anyone beyond the story, and Jesus’ own sentence in 12:21 does not use it.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The explanation stays with its account. The words of Luke 12:15 and 12:21 belong to Luke’s account of this day. The word “covetousness” in 12:15 and the word “treasure” in 12:21 do not become the same thing because they stand on either side of the story, and neither is made to supply an identification the other leaves out. Jesus’ words about treasure in 12:33–34 are spoken to His disciples after the story and are read in their own place.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It repeats none of Jesus’ identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus tells of a rich man whose ground brought forth plentifully, whose plan stops at his barns and his ease, and whose soul is required that night. He closes by saying that so is he who lays up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God. The comparison is made at the level of what the man does and what happens to him.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Abundance is not what a life consists in.** The warning of 12:15 and the story that follows put the man’s abundance beside the end of his night.',
    '**The plan leaves God out until God speaks.** The man speaks “within himself” and to his soul, and God is named in the story only when He speaks.',
    '**The end comes unannounced.** “This night” breaks into a plan that counted on “many years.”',
    '**The goods outlast the man’s hold on them.** God’s question, “whose shall those things be?”, is left standing, and the story gives no answer.',
    '**Laying up for oneself is set against being rich toward God.** Jesus puts the two side by side in His closing words.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were one of the company who asked about an inheritance, the multitude that was gathered, and the disciples to whom Jesus had been speaking (12:1, 13). They were meant to understand, from the story and the words around it, that covetousness is a danger, that a man’s life is not measured by the abundance of what he has, and that laying up treasure for oneself while not being rich toward God ends as the rich man’s night ended. These are comparisons about that ministry, and they are not about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.4 What this section does not do. It does not say who the rich man is, or what the ground, the barns, the goods or the soul is. It does not say what being rich toward God is. It does not turn planning, building, laying up or being rich toward God into steps by which a person is saved. It draws no meaning from another parable into this story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The Rich Fool is told in Luke 12 after a request about an inheritance. It is kept apart here from three neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 10:25–37', '“A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho”', 'The Good Samaritan. Told after a lawyer’s question about eternal life and a neighbour, with a man who falls among thieves and a Samaritan who helps. It has its own study. The rich man is not read into it and it is not read into him.'],
    ['Luke 16:1–13', '“There was a certain rich man, which had a steward”', 'The Shrewd Manager. A rich man and his steward, told to the disciples in Luke 16, with its own setting and its own study. The two rich men are not the same man.'],
    ['Luke 16:19–31', '“There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple”', 'The Rich Man and Lazarus. Another rich man, in another chapter, with a beggar at his gate. It has its own study. Neither story explains the other.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The Rich Fool is told in a multitude after a request about an inheritance and a warning against covetousness (12:13–15). The Good Samaritan is told after a lawyer’s question (10:25–29). The Shrewd Manager and the Rich Man and Lazarus are told in Luke 16, the first to His disciples (16:1) and the second after words about the Pharisees (16:14–15). Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The Rich Fool has a certain rich man, his ground, his barns, his soul and God who speaks. The Good Samaritan has a man, thieves, a priest, a Levite, a Samaritan and an innkeeper. The Shrewd Manager has a rich man, a steward and debtors. The Rich Man and Lazarus has a rich man, a beggar, Abraham and the brothers. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The Rich Fool moves from a plentiful harvest to a plan, to words to the soul, to God’s word and to a question. The Good Samaritan moves from a journey to a wound to help. The Shrewd Manager moves from an accusation to a reckoning to debts. The Rich Man and Lazarus moves from a gate to two deaths to a conversation. None of those movements is read into the Rich Fool.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “a certain rich man” of Luke 12:16 is not “a certain rich man” of Luke 16:1 or 16:19, and the same phrase in different stories does not make one man. Second, God’s speech inside this story (12:20) has no counterpart in the other three stories and is not used to explain them. Third, “this night thy soul shall be required of thee” is not read as a statement about what happens to the dead in Luke 16:22–23, and neither passage is used to explain the other.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. In Luke 12, Jesus speaks in a gathered multitude, first to His disciples (12:1), then to one of the company who asked about an inheritance (12:13), and then to “them” (12:15–16). He speaks in the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The story of the rich man was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 12:13–21 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The rich man’s plan, his barns, his night and God’s question are not a description of the Body of Christ. The story is not read as a universal picture of every person who has goods. “Not rich toward God” and “layeth up treasure for himself” are Jesus’ words in His earthly ministry, and they are not carried over as a measure of the Body’s standing. The planning, the building, the laying up and the being rich toward God are actions in a story and in a saying, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). The soul required that night is not the Body’s destiny, and the story is not read as a statement about how any person is saved or lost.'))
A(('pk', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparison shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of watchfulness.** Jesus says, “Take heed, and beware of covetousness.” A reader learns to watch for covetousness in himself, and not only in the man who asked.',
    '**A lesson of measure.** “A man’s life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth.” A reader learns not to measure a life by what it holds.',
    '**A lesson of planning under a limit.** The man’s plan counts on “many years,” and the night ends it. A reader learns that his own plans are made under a limit he does not control.',
    '**A lesson of accountability for goods.** God asks, “whose shall those things be?” A reader learns to ask whose his things will be, and what he is doing with them now.',
    '**A lesson of where the treasure is laid.** Jesus sets “treasure for himself” beside being “rich toward God.” A reader learns to weigh where his treasure is being laid up.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the rich man, or that the Body’s goods are the man’s goods, or that the Body’s standing depends on how its goods are held. They are principles drawn from what the story shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells of a certain rich man whose ground brought forth plentifully. The man thinks within himself that he has no room for his fruits, plans to pull down his barns and build greater, and means to say to his soul that it has much goods laid up for many years and may take its ease. God speaks that night: “Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?” Before the story Jesus says to beware of covetousness, because a man’s life does not consist in the abundance of the things he possesses. After it He says that so is he who lays up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God (Luke 12:15, 21). Jesus names nothing else in the story. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the rich man is, what the barns or the soul stand for, or that makes planning, laying up or being rich toward God a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a plentiful ground, a question about room, a plan for greater barns, words to the soul, God’s word that night, and a question that is left standing.',
    '**The meaning:** a life does not consist in abundance, and a man who lays up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God comes to the end the story shows.',
    '**The boundaries:** only covetousness, a man’s life, abundance, treasure laid up for himself and being rich toward God are named; the man, the ground, the barns, the goods and the soul are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** watchfulness, measure, planning under a limit, accountability for goods and where treasure is laid, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

